-- #3317 — THE PRICE OF ROUTING A LET-BOUND OPERATOR, PINNED ON ALL THREE VERBS.
--
-- An operator on the method seam (`==` here) is a method occurrence in surface
-- syntax, so `let e = (==)` now carries the same constraint `let e = eq` does: a
-- local binding cannot be dict-abstracted, so it cannot be polymorphic in the type
-- the instance is chosen from. Used at ONE type it is legal and reaches the
-- instance; used at two, as here (`Int` and `K`), typecheck owes a located
-- T-LOCAL-CONSTRAINED-MONO naming the binding, both types, the interface and the
-- hoist-to-top-level remedy.
--
-- The rejection has to be a DIAGNOSTIC on all three verbs, which is what this
-- corpus adds over an ordinary typecheck fixture. Before #3317 this program was
-- ACCEPTED by all three and then answered `False` for `e (K 1 9) (K 1 8)` from the
-- shallow structural comparison, while the infix `K 1 9 == K 1 8` beside it answered
-- `True` from the instance. A regression that restores the old acceptance would
-- restore that silent disagreement, so this fixture is the acceptance half of the
-- routing fix, not a diagnostics test.
--
-- Verdict: REJECT.
data K = K Int Int

impl Eq K where
  eq (K a _) (K b _) = a == b

main =
  let e = (==)
  let onInts = e 1 1
  let onK = e (K 1 9) (K 1 8)
  println (debug (onInts, onK))
